world war two was fought by every major  middle power in the world and very few  parts of it were left unscarred there is  one notable exception to this the  Republic of Turkey now whilst they did  nominally declare war in 1945 just as  Germany was about to fall its soldiers  never saw combat and to pretty frankly  turkey still wasn't too keen on joining  the war so how did turkey managed to  stay out of the war for so long and even  when it did join it only did so under  direct pressure and because without  doing so it would be sidelined in the  post-war world it's crucial to remember  that in the years leading up to World  War two Turkey was still recovering from  its war of independence which did  tremendous damage to the country this  was followed by major reforms in a  period of the country's first president  Mustafa Kemal atatürk attempting to  strengthen turkeys global position  Turkey's position was a strange one  since in 1939 you were often either a  great power or a colony and Turkey was  neither as such it was able to forge its  own course and was capable of resisting  its larger neighbors in a way that many  European nations couldn't in October of  1939 shortly after the outbreak of the  war Turkey signed a mutual assistance  pact with Britain and France which made  it pretty clear that Turkey lent towards  the Allied camp the reason why turkey  was less friendly towards the axis  wasn't because of some dislike for  Germany since the two nations actually  had good relations it was in fact down  to turkish italian tensions due to  mussolini wanting to dominate the  Mediterranean so the mutual assistance  pact wasn't the same as joining the  Allies it saw Britain give turkey a lot  of money to modernize and increase the  size of its army turkey was still  determined to stay out of the war though  as demonstrated by President T nany when  he stated that for us war is out of the  question unless we are left absolutely  no choice this is why despite Britain 